% =======================================================
\section{Continuous Magnetic Hard-Iron Correction}
\label{sec:mag-hard-iron}

The startup alignment of Sec.~\ref{sec:mag-two-stage} delivers a magnetic world
reference and a heading gauge, and once it has run, the residual heading error
of this filter is no longer an attitude-tracking error.  It is a property of
the reference the alignment learned.  This section states what that error is,
why no startup window can remove it, and what the deployed filter does about
it: a continuously running, exogenous estimator of the body-fixed magnetometer
offset, whose answer is applied to the measurement stream rather than to any
attitude state.

\subsection{The standing error is a gauge, not a tracking error}
\label{sec:mag-gauge}

On the PM--Stokes $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ record with the correction disabled, the
scored yaw error has mean \SI{2.146}{\degree} and standard deviation
\SI{0.265}{\degree} over the \SI{900}{s} window.  The filter is not failing to
track heading; it is pointing \SI{2.1}{\degree} away from magnetic north for
the whole record and holding that direction steadily.

The reason is visible in what the alignment averages.  With $\mat A_i$ the
yaw-stripped body-to-level rotation at magnetometer sample $i$, a body-fixed
offset $\vct b_m$ and the level-frame field $\vct B_\ell$, the measurement is
\begin{equation}
\vct m_i=\mat A_i^{\top}\vct B_\ell+\vct b_m+\vct n_i ,
\label{eq:mag-offset-model}
\end{equation}
so the average the acquisition declares to be north is
\begin{equation}
\bar{\vct w}
:=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i \mat A_i\vct m_i
=\vct B_\ell+\bar{\mat A}\,\vct b_m ,
\qquad
\bar{\mat A}:=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i \mat A_i .
\label{eq:mag-ref-average}
\end{equation}
For a hull at fixed heading $\bar{\mat A}\approx\Id$, and the learned north is
the direction of $\vct B_\ell+\vct b_m$ rather than of $\vct B_\ell$.  The
angle between them is the standing yaw error.  The simulator's hard-iron
residual is drawn from a \SI{2.0}{\micro\tesla} half-range per axis
(Table~\ref{tab:sim_constants}); the shipped draw is \SI{1.26}{\micro\tesla} in
norm with a horizontal part of \SI{0.83}{\micro\tesla}.  Against the simulated
field --- \SI{52}{\micro\tesla} total at \SI{66.5}{\degree} inclination, so
\SI{20.7}{\micro\tesla} horizontal --- that is an angle of \SI{2.3}{\degree},
which is the standing error the filter reports, to the accuracy this
small-angle argument deserves.

Nothing downstream can recover it, because the world frame \emph{is} that
direction: the yaw error is a gauge choice, and every consistent estimator
inside the filter agrees with it.  Removing each part of the simulator's
magnetometer error with simulator truth, as a non-deployable oracle
diagnostic, locates it unambiguously.  Over the eight records, yaw RMS mean and
worst are \SI{1.835}{\degree}/\SI{2.162}{\degree} uncorrected,
\SI{1.811}{\degree}/\SI{2.121}{\degree} with soft iron and misalignment removed
perfectly, \SI{0.496}{\degree}/\SI{1.084}{\degree} with the hard iron removed
perfectly, and \SI{0.479}{\degree}/\SI{1.059}{\degree} with both.  On the
shipped draw essentially all of the standing error is the offset, and perfect
knowledge of it would leave about half a degree.

\subsection{Why a startup window cannot identify the offset}
\label{sec:mag-excitation}

Estimating $\vct b_m$ jointly with $\vct B_\ell$ from \eqref{eq:mag-offset-model}
is only possible through attitude change: at a single attitude an offset and a
rotated world field produce the same reading.  Eliminating $\vct B_\ell$
analytically from the weighted normal equations of \eqref{eq:mag-offset-model}
leaves
\begin{equation}
\underbrace{\left(\Id-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\mat A}\right)}_{=:\;\mat M}
\vct b_m
=\bar{\vct m}-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\vct w},
\qquad
\bar{\vct m}:=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i\vct m_i ,
\label{eq:mag-normal}
\end{equation}
so the world field never becomes a filter state and $\mat M$ alone decides what
is identifiable.  For roll and pitch of standard deviation $\sigma_r$,
$\sigma_p$ about a fixed heading,
\begin{equation}
\mat M\approx\diag\!\left(\sigma_p^{2},\;\sigma_r^{2},\;
\sigma_r^{2}+\sigma_p^{2}\right)
\label{eq:mag-excitation}
\end{equation}
to second order, which is of order $10^{-3}$ for a hull working through a few
degrees.  A \SI{15}{s} acquisition window buys a few wave periods of that, and
the fix is not a longer window: the deficiency is in the conditioning of
\eqref{eq:mag-normal}, not in the sample count.  The window has to stay open.

\subsection{Estimator}
\label{sec:mag-hi-estimator}

The deployed filter therefore runs the accumulation for the whole life of the
record.  Exponentially weighted sufficient statistics of $(\mat A_i,\vct m_i)$
with memory \SI{600}{s} are maintained at magnetometer rate, and
\eqref{eq:mag-normal} is solved once per second.  Two properties make the
estimator exogenous with respect to the MEKF in the same sense as the tuner of
Sec.~\ref{sec:startup-policy}: the rotation is the yaw-stripped tilt of the
private Mahony observer, built from gravity alone, and the magnetometer stream
is the raw one, not the stream with the estimator's own answer already
subtracted.  No MEKF state is read, and no loop is closed through the filter.

Stripping yaw from the accumulation frame does two things.  It keeps the
observer's unobservable, drifting heading out of the statistics.  And it ties
the frame to the hull's own heading, so a hull that turns makes $\vct B_\ell$
move within the frame and raises the model residual immediately, which is what
the residual gate below is for.

\paragraph{Regularization} The solve is ridge-regularized, and the ridge prices
model error rather than sample noise.  Inverting a matrix of order $10^{-3}$
multiplies whatever \eqref{eq:mag-offset-model} does not carry by up to a
thousand, and what it does not carry is soft iron and sensor misalignment ---
both modulated by the very tilt the offset is read from, so neither averages
away as the record lengthens.  An unregularized fit does not converge on the
offset; it converges on the offset plus an aliased image of the distortion,
which over the full \SI{1200}{s} against truth attitude returns about
$1.8\times$ the true offset on every well-excited record and overshoots the
correction into a heading error of the opposite sign.

The size of that aliased error is of order $\varepsilon\lVert\vct B\rVert$ ---
the fractional distortion times the field --- largely independently of sea
state, because it enters the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:mag-normal} through
the same tilt that carries the signal.  A hull working through
\SI{30}{\degree} is not fitting a cleaner offset than one working through five;
it is fitting the same wrong one with more confidence.  A fixed ridge therefore
shrinks hardest exactly where the fit is most trustworthy.  The deployed ridge
is
\begin{equation}
\left(\mat M+\left(\rho_0+\rho_1\,\tfrac{1}{3}\operatorname{tr}\mat M\right)
\Id\right)\hat{\vct b}_m=\bar{\vct m}-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\vct w},
\label{eq:mag-ridge}
\end{equation}
with $\rho_1=0.5$ scaling with the mean excitation so that the fraction of the
offset returned is a property of the sensor rather than of the weather, and
$\rho_0=\num{4e-3}$ as an absolute floor for a window with almost no excitation
at all.  A unit test pins the difference: replaying one offset and one
distortion through a small and a large sea, the floor alone returns \num{0.27}
and \num{0.88} of the offset, while \eqref{eq:mag-ridge} returns \num{0.24} and
\num{0.66}.

\paragraph{Validity gates} An estimate is offered only when the window has
reached \num{500} effective samples and an excitation
$N\lambda_{\min}(\mat M)\geq 2$, when the fitted offset is below \pct{35} of
the measured field norm, and when the weighted residual of
$\mat A_i\vct m_i=\vct B_\ell+\mat A_i\vct b_m$ is at or below
\SI{3}{\micro\tesla} RMS.  The excitation gate answers only whether the hull
has moved at all --- how much of a poorly excited window survives is
\eqref{eq:mag-ridge}'s business, and a gate set high enough to act as a second
shrinkage stands the estimator down in exactly the calm seas where the standing
error is largest.

\subsection{Applying the estimate without preserving the gauge}
\label{sec:mag-hi-apply}

The obvious way to stay self-consistent is to subtract $\hat{\vct b}_m$ from
every sample and subtract the matching $\bar{\mat A}\hat{\vct b}_m$ from the
reference, per \eqref{eq:mag-ref-average}.  That is exactly wrong, and it is a
measurable no-op: the innovation
\begin{equation}
\vct m-\hat{\vct b}_m
-\mat A^{\top}\!\left(\bar{\vct w}-\bar{\mat A}\hat{\vct b}_m\right)
\label{eq:mag-noop}
\end{equation}
is identical to the uncorrected one at the attitude the filter already holds,
because $\mat A^{\top}\bar{\mat A}\approx\Id$ for a hull at fixed heading.
Forcing a \SI{10}{\micro\tesla} offset through that path moves the scored yaw by
\SI{0.3}{\degree}.  A change that preserves the innovation preserves the gauge,
and the gauge is the error being removed.

What the filter does instead is to break that identity deliberately:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em]
  \item the offset is subtracted from the magnetometer stream, slewed toward
        the fitted value with a \SI{45}{s} time constant so the measurement
        model never steps; and
  \item the world reference stays in the canonical form the acquisition
        produced --- horizontal magnitude on $+X$, vertical below it --- with
        only its magnitude and vertical component moved, and only by the
        \emph{delta} that the applied offset makes to them, both evaluated
        against the statistics as they stand.
\end{itemize}

The corrected field then implies a different heading than the reference
asserts, and the magnetometer update of Sec.~\ref{sec:measurement_model} walks
yaw onto it over its own time constant.  No attitude state is written and no
covariance is reset: the correction is a change of measurement-model
parameters, which is what keeps it inside the Live recursion analyzed in
Sec.~\ref{app:iss-stability} rather than alongside the one-time reconfiguration
events that are excluded from it.

Taking the magnitude and dip as a delta, rather than adopting the estimator's
own window wholesale, is also not cosmetic.  That window is longer and less
selective than the one the acquisition gated, and adopting its magnitude and
dip costs about a fifth of the roll accuracy on the moderate seas while the
offset correction itself costs none.  With the delta form, an estimator that
never validates leaves every scored metric bit-identical.  Accumulation begins
with the first magnetometer sample, but nothing is applied until the two-stage
acquisition has finished, so first heading, handoff and refinement are exactly
as Sec.~\ref{sec:mag-two-stage} describes them.

\subsection{Measured effect}
\label{sec:mag-hi-results}

Table~\ref{tab:mag-hi-yaw} reports the deterministic protocol of
Sec.~\ref{sec:sim-and-eval} --- one realization per record, default seeds, the
final \SI{900}{s} of a \SI{1200}{s} replay, and not the ten-seed study --- with
the correction disabled and enabled.  Every record improves, the eight-record mean falls from
\SI{1.835}{\degree} to \SI{0.822}{\degree}, and the worst record falls from
\SI{2.162}{\degree} to \SI{1.063}{\degree}, which is close to the
\SI{1.059}{\degree} the oracle of Sec.~\ref{sec:mag-gauge} obtains with both
error sources removed perfectly.

