Pitch improves slightly, for the same reason yaw does --- the reference's dip
moves with the offset --- while roll and the displacement channels do not move:
mean roll is \SI{0.339}{\degree} against \SI{0.340}{\degree}, and mean 3D
displacement RMS \SI{0.6212}{m} against \SI{0.6199}{m}.  The vertical channel
is unchanged to the precision the deterministic table reports.  The
regression sentinel on yaw is re-derived accordingly, from \SI{2.2}{\degree} to
\SI{1.1}{\degree}; the matched ablation is the disabled correction, and it now
exceeds that sentinel, as a limit fitted to the deployed configuration should.

\subsection{What the estimator cannot do}
\label{sec:mag-hi-limits}

The correction removes one of the two things that shift the gauge, and there is
no local signal that says which one the filter is facing.  Repeating the
deterministic protocol over five draws of the magnetometer calibration
(\texttt{W3D\_INIT\_SEED} of the shipped default, \num{11}, \num{23}, \num{202}
and \num{3033}, which redraw the offset, the scale and cross-axis terms and the
misalignment together), \num{40} record/draw pairs, the eight-record mean yaw
falls from \SI{2.810}{\degree} to \SI{2.124}{\degree} and the worst pair from
\SI{5.258}{\degree} to \SI{4.510}{\degree}.  Ten of the forty are worse with
the correction on, and all ten belong to two draws --- the two whose standing
error is not offset-dominated.  On one of them the mean uncorrected yaw is
already \SI{0.738}{\degree}, half that of any other draw: there is little for a
hard-iron estimator to win, and the fraction of the distortion it aliases into
its answer moves the gauge the wrong way.  On the other, draw \num{23}, the
same oracle diagnostic says why --- removing its hard iron perfectly takes yaw
from \SI{2.98}{\degree} to \SI{2.74}{\degree}, while removing its soft iron and
misalignment perfectly takes it to \SI{1.02}{\degree}.

Neither case is detectable from inside the filter.  The aliased part of the
distortion is, by construction, the part the model \emph{explains}, so it does
not raise the residual the validity gate watches: the estimator reports
\SI{1.683}{\micro\tesla} on the shipped draw and \SI{1.685}{\micro\tesla} on
draw \num{23}, against a white-noise floor of \SI{1.386}{\micro\tesla} --- the
per-axis \SI{0.80}{\micro\tesla} of Table~\ref{tab:sim_constants} over three
axes.  The residual gate catches a turning hull, not a misaligned sensor.

Two consequences are worth stating plainly.  The shrinkage of
\eqref{eq:mag-ridge} is not a tuning convenience but the bound on that failure
mode, which is why the ridge is left large enough to give up real accuracy on
the favourable draws.  And the way to actually fix a misalignment-dominated
draw is to identify the distortion, which requires heading excitation these
fixed-heading records do not contain.  The estimator stands itself down on a
turn today rather than exploiting it; using the turn is the obvious next piece
of work and is deliberately not attempted here.
